---
import { getEntry } from 'astro:content';
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import CaseHeader from '../../components/CaseHeader.astro';
import CaseSections from '../../components/CaseSections.astro';
import Section from '../../components/Section.astro';
import MarginNote from '../../components/MarginNote.astro';
import EditView from '../../components/EditView.astro';
import Figure from '../../components/Figure.astro';
import Compare from '../../components/Compare.astro';
import Gallery from '../../components/Gallery.astro';
import Ledger from '../../components/Ledger.astro';
import Persona from '../../components/Persona.astro';
import Pager from '../../components/Pager.astro';
import { img } from '../../data/images';

const project = (await getEntry('projects', 'ladwp-turf-replacement'))!;
const la = (f: string) => img('ladwp-turf-replacement', f);

const updates = [
  { issue: 'Hierarchy', response: 'Headings in the HTML didn’t follow an order. I set each heading level by importance, position and size, which also helps screen readers.' },
  { issue: 'Bullet points', response: 'Some “bullets” were characters typed inside a paragraph. I rebuilt them as real list items.' },
  { issue: 'Style', response: 'Some styles were inconsistent; the ® symbol, for example, wasn’t superscripted everywhere. I made them consistent.' },
  { issue: 'Banner', response: 'The hero banner now states the program’s purpose on the homepage, and names the current page everywhere else.' },
  { issue: 'Images', response: 'Images no longer clash with the text, and they show what the program is for.' },
  { issue: 'Alternate text', response: 'Every image has alt text, for screen readers and for when images fail to load.' },
  { issue: 'Localization', response: 'I added a full Spanish version of the site, reachable from a button in the header, an English–Spanish toggle on every page, and the footer.' },
];
---
<Base title={project.data.title} description={project.data.summary} current="work">
  <article class="wrap">
    <CaseHeader project={project} />
    <CaseSections>

    <Section id="context" title="Context">
      <div class="col-text prose">
        <p>
          Water is limited in Los Angeles, and a lot of it goes on lawns of plants that aren’t native to California.
          The Los Angeles Department of Water and Power (LADWP) wants to reduce that.
        </p>
        <p>
          The Turf Replacement Design website lets residents sign up for help replacing their lawns with California-native plants,
          which naturally need less water.
        </p>
      </div>
      <MarginNote label="Before launch">I worked on the site before it launched. Some content on the live site has changed since.</MarginNote>
    </Section>

    <Section id="persona" title="Persona">
      <div class="col-wide two-up">
        <Persona
          name="Monica"
          age={24}
          status="Married"
          occupation="Visual artist"
          location="Los Angeles, California"
          quote="I love it when I just sign up and I’m done! The process already started."
          bio="Loves color. Works from home often and looks after her garden. Wants to keep costs down while keeping a house as artistic as she is."
          needs="A professional designer who can suggest native plants without lowering her standards for how the garden looks."
          pains="Conflicting information: one process on the website, another in a pamphlet, a third on another site. She doesn’t want to chase it down."
        />
        <div class="persona-gloss prose">
          <p class="meta">Connection</p>
          <p>
            Monica’s pain point lines up with one of the changes below: on the Program Steps page I removed information that was
            already elsewhere on the site or in the application.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </Section>

    <Section id="global" title="Global updates">
      <div class="col-text prose">
        <p>
          The site started from a template built for a similar purpose. When I got to work on the site and its updates, I made these
          changes across all of it.
        </p>
      </div>
      <Ledger caption="Changes made across the whole site" headers={['Area', 'What I changed']} rows={updates} />
    </Section>

    <Section id="hyperlinking" title="Hyperlinking">
      <div class="col-text prose">
        <p>
          Many sentences linked only one to three words, usually a noun. I extended each link across the whole phrase. A longer link
          tells you why you’re being sent somewhere, and it’s easier to click.
        </p>
      </div>
    </Section>

    <Section id="steps" title="Program steps">
      <div class="col-text prose">
        <p>
          Program Steps is where people learn what applying looks like and how the garden design process unfolds. Over several
          iterations the team reduced the number of steps, which made the process simpler for everyone.
        </p>
        <p>
          I added a link to a video and gave it the center of the page, in larger text. I removed information that already lived
          elsewhere on the site or in the application, gave the step titles one consistent format, and used images and white space
          so the steps read as a sequence.
        </p>
      </div>
      <Figure src={la('LA_WP_dept_1.png')} alt="Program Steps page: 1) Submit Your Application, 2) Receive Approval and Request for Design Preferences, 3) Meet with the Designer, 4) Receive the Design Plans, each with a short paragraph and a garden photo." size="margin" />
    </Section>

    <Section id="application" title="Application">
      <div class="col-text prose">
        <p>
          As a team we went through the application asking what was truly needed to complete it. It went from five pages to four,
          and most pages got shorter. I added photos that show which kinds of lawn qualify.
        </p>
        <p>
          I also wrote alert messages that tell people why they can’t continue: a question left blank, an answer in the wrong format,
          or an answer that makes them ineligible.
        </p>
      </div>
      <Gallery
        id="application"
        label="Application screens"
        items={[
          { src: la('LA_WP_dept_3.png'), alt: 'Application page: terms to agree to, then "What does your Turf Area look like?" with two photo choices, green grass and bare dirt.', caption: 'Photo choices show which lawns qualify.' },
          { src: la('LA_WP_dept_2.png'), alt: 'Application page "Provide Contact Information" with name, phone, email and address fields.', caption: 'Contact information page.' },
          { src: la('LA_WP_dept_11.png'), alt: 'A variant of the contact information page with the same fields.', caption: 'Contact information, variant.' },
        ]}
      />
    </Section>

    <Section id="troubleshooting" title="Troubleshooting">
      <div class="col-text prose">
        <p>
          The application is embedded in the site as a widget, and that caused problems for some people. I added a tip before the
          Apply button and above the application itself. It recommends Chrome, links to Chrome’s instructions for clearing the cache,
          and links to the form in its own window.
        </p>
      </div>
      <figure class="col-wide tip">
        <blockquote>
          <p>
            It’s recommended to use the Chrome browser to access the form. If you have problems accessing the form, please disable your
            ad blockers and <u>clear your cache</u>. If trouble still persists, please open <u>the form in its own window</u>.
          </p>
        </blockquote>
        <figcaption class="meta">The tip, with its links underlined.</figcaption>
      </figure>
    </Section>

    <Section id="email" title="Confirmation email" kicker="Writing · HTML">
      <div class="col-text prose">
        <p>
          After applying, people get an email with their confirmation number and a copy of their answers. It was plain text.
          I rebuilt it in HTML and changed the subject line.
        </p>
      </div>
      <EditView
        size="small"
        parts={[
          { t: 'LADWP ' },
          { del: 'Turf Replacement Design Services ' },
          { t: '- John Test 51 - ' },
          { ins: 'Turf Replacement Design Services - ' },
          { t: 'Application in Review' },
        ]}
        caption="Subject line, with a test applicant"
        note="The applicant’s name and confirmation number now come before the program name, so they’re visible sooner in an inbox."
      />
      <Compare
        a={{ src: la('LA_WP_dept_6.png'), label: 'Before: plain text', alt: 'Plain-text confirmation email: paragraphs of text followed by the applicant’s answers as unformatted lines like "Are you an LADWP customer? yes".' }}
        b={{ src: la('LA_WP_dept_7.png'), label: 'After: HTML', alt: 'HTML confirmation email: LADWP logo, "Application Confirmation Receipt" heading, then contact details and questionnaire answers in two-column tables with alternating row shading.' }}
      />
      <div class="col-text prose">
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Bold</strong> for titles and for the questions the applicant answered.</li>
          <li><strong>A two-column table</strong>, question and answer, with alternating row colors for legibility.</li>
          <li><strong>Dividers</strong> between header, tables and footer.</li>
          <li><strong>The logo</strong> in the header and a website link in the footer, so people know who sent it and can click through.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </Section>

    <Section id="retrospective" title="Retrospective">
      <div class="col-text prose">
        <p>
          Getting people to follow instructions was a challenge, and ongoing changes kept requiring updates across the whole site.
          I would have liked to write the program’s social media too. Still, people signed up for the garden redesign, and they liked
          that it was straightforward and mostly in one place.
        </p>
        <p>
          I enjoyed the localization; I’m always happy to work on translation and on the needs of people from other cultures.
          I also enjoyed the HTML and CSS work, especially the before and after of the confirmation email, where markup made a
          real difference to the people receiving it.
        </p>
      </div>
    </Section>
    </CaseSections>
  </article>
  <Pager id={project.id} />
</Base>

<style>
  .persona-gloss { align-self: end; border-left: 3px solid var(--highlight); padding-left: var(--space-3); font-size: var(--step--1); }
  .tip { margin: var(--space-3) 0; }
  .tip blockquote {
    margin: 0;
    padding: var(--space-4);
    background: var(--paper-sunk);
    border-left: 3px solid var(--ink);
    font-family: var(--sans);
    font-size: var(--step-0);
    line-height: 1.6;
  }
  .tip u { text-decoration-color: var(--ink-muted); }
  .tip figcaption { margin-top: var(--space-2); }
</style>
